media: camss: SDM845 camera subsystem support (backport, 5.10)

The OnePlus 6 kernel (5.10) has CAMSS only for VFE 4.1/4.7 (MSM8916/MSM8996): no CSID/VFE "Titan 170",
the SDM845 camera subsystem (upstream since 5.13), so there is no camera at all. This replaces
drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss with the driver of the sdm845-mainline tree (codeberg.org/sdm845/linux,
branch sdm845-next, the 7.4-rc1 based tree on which the OnePlus 6 cameras run with mainline Linux),
reduced to SDM845 and ported down to the 5.10 media core:
- CSIPHY 3-phase (D-PHY and C-PHY, the main camera's IMX519 is C-PHY), CSID gen2, VFE 17x, video nodes;
  the 2026 VFE 17x fixes (write master selection, dropped buffer done, reset while streaming) and the
  link-rate clamp for CSIPHY timers are included.
- Only SDM845 resources and hardware versions are kept; the PHY-API CSIPHY mode, the common TPG block
  and ISPIF are left out (not used on SDM845).
- camss-compat.h maps the newer API onto 5.10 (subdev state -> pad config, async connection -> async subdev,
  v4l2_get_link_freq, regulator init loads); vb2 has no prepare_streaming on 5.10, so the pipeline is powered
  in start/stop_streaming; include/media/mipi-csi2.h (data type ids) is added.
- Each sensor is linked to its CSIPHY and the subdev nodes are created when that sensor binds, not only
  when all sensors of the device tree have bound: the OnePlus 6 has three sensors with separate drivers, and
  one that fails to probe must not hide the others.
- Bring-up aid, off by default: `enable_gcc_axi=1` keeps the GCC camera AXI clock on while CAMSS is bound
  (upstream never enables it for SDM845; if the VFE write masters stall here, this rules the clock out).
The driver is a module (CONFIG_VIDEO_QCOM_CAMSS=m); the built-in kernel is unchanged.

